      "is_timeout": false,
      "backend": "review-default"
    },
    {
      "id": "l1-expert-5859725e41c3420b",
      "fingerprint": "5859725e41c3420b",
      "source": "L1",
      "disposition": "CONFIRMED",
      "file": "src/code_forge/context_sources.py",
      "line_range": [
        48,
        48
      ],
      "description": "[expert] Mutable dict fields on frozen FactRow break its hashability contract. FactRow is decorated @dataclass(frozen=True), which auto-generates __hash__. The new snippets and enclosing fields are plain dict (not Mapping or MappingProxyType), so any attempt to hash a FactRow instance\u2014including putting it in a set or using it as a dict key\u2014will raise TypeError: unhashable type: 'dict'. Before this change all FactRow fields were str or Optional[int], so the class was safely hashable. Either annotate the fields as Mapping[str, str] (with an explicit cast or MappingProxyType at construction) or add eq=False to the field() calls if they should not participate in __eq__/__hash__.",
      "error": null,
      "anchor": null,
      "evidence_files": null,
      "is_timeout": false,
      "backend": "review-default"
    },
    {
      "id": "l1-expert-af12b0f6894fd738",
      "fingerprint": "af12b0f6894fd738",
      "source": "L1",
      "disposition": "UNCERTAIN",
      "file": "src/code_forge/context_sources.py",
      "line_range": [
        322,
        322
      ],
      "description": "[expert] _self_reads and _readers silently swallow OSError/SubprocessError and return an empty list. The module docstring (line 13-14) states 'a provider that raises is recorded as an error, not swallowed into an empty table', yet these helper methods do exactly that: a missing git binary or permission error produces zero readers with no error signal. The gather() function has an exception recorder that would surface such errors if they propagated. Consider letting these exceptions rise (or logging them into a side-channel) so the caller can distinguish 'no readers found' from 'git grep unavailable'.",
      "error": null,
      "anchor": null,
      "evidence_files": null,
      "is_timeout": false,
      "backend": "review-default"
    },
    {
      "id": "l1-adversarial-98f985bcaf6517aa",
      "fingerprint": "98f985bcaf6517aa",
      "source": "L1",
      "disposition": "DISMISSED",
      "file": "src/code_forge/context_sources.py",
      "line_range": [
        461,
        461
      ],
      "description": "[adversarial] The else branch in `_dropped_parameters_by_file` unconditionally strips the first character via `line[1:]`, relying on the assumption that every non-`-`/non-`+` line that reaches the else branch starts with a space (i.e. is a context line). While all other prefix types (`---`, `+++`, `@@`, `diff`, `index`) are filtered earlier, any future addition of a filter or a malformed diff with a bare line would have its first character silently stripped. The idiomatic and defensive pattern is `line[1:] if line.startswith(' ') else line` (or equivalently `line[1:] if line.startswith(' ') else line`). This avoids a silent data-corruption class of bug if the early filters change.",
      "error": null,
      "anchor": null,
      "evidence_files": null,
      "is_timeout": false,
      "backend": "review-default"
    },
    {
      "id": "l1-qodo-52c68a90d3dffaa2",
